    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "   \nWe start with the given condition that \\( n \\) leaves a remainder of 1 when divided by 6. Mathematically, this can be expressed as:\n\\[\nn \\equiv 1 \\pmod{6}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to determine the remainder when \\( n + 2010 \\) is divided by 6. Let's denote this remainder by \\( r \\). Therefore, we have:\n\\[\nn + 2010 \\equiv r \\pmod{6}\n\\]\n\nSince \\( n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{6} \\), we can substitute \\( n \\) in the expression \\( n + 2010 \\):\n\\[\nn + 2010 \\equiv 1 + 2010 \\pmod{6}\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to compute \\( 1 + 2010 \\mod 6 \\). First, we find the remainder when 2010 is divided by 6. We can do this by performing the division:\n\\[\n2010 \\div 6 = 335 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of} \\quad 0\n\\]\nThis means:\n\\[\n2010 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{6}\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\\[\n1 + 2010 \\equiv 1 + 0 \\pmod{6}\n\\]\n\\[\n1 + 2010 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{6}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the remainder when \\( n + 2010 \\) is divided by 6 is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]",
